        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nEncode and decode an augmented matrix without losing zero coefficients, signs or the distinction between data and unknowns.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview arithmetic with signed numbers, substitution, ordered pairs and the distributive law. Keep variable names and units consistent when translating a context into equations.\n\n## Intuition first\nA table can store the fixed numbers in a collection of equations while leaving repeated variable names out. The table is useful only if everyone agrees on what each column means.\n\n## Why this works\nFix the unknown order before writing a coefficient matrix. Each equation contributes one row, and each unknown contributes one coefficient column. Missing terms have coefficient zero; they do not delete a column. The right-hand constants form a separate vector. An augmented matrix attaches that vector as one extra column, so the vertical separator records a difference of meaning rather than an extra algebraic operation.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw three labelled coefficient columns x,y,z and a separated constants column. Move each equation’s coefficients into its row, keeping the columns fixed even where a term is absent.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise From equations to augmented matrices\nWrite the augmented matrix for $2x-y+3z=7$ and $x+4y-2z=-5$ in variable order x,y,z. Decode the matrix $\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}3&0&8\\\\-2&5&1\\end{array}\\right]$ in variable order u,v.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe first augmented matrix is $\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2&-1&3&7\\\\1&4&-2&-5\\end{array}\\right]$. Its coefficient matrix has two rows and three columns, while the augmented matrix has four columns. The second matrix represents $3u=8$ and $-2u+5v=1$. The zero coefficient preserves the place of v in the first row; it is not an instruction to change variable order.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nMatrix size and column meaning should be checked before elimination. An augmented column stores constants; it is not an additional unknown.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not move a constant into a coefficient column. Do not omit zeros for absent terms. The separator is visual notation, not a determinant bar.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nHow many columns does an augmented matrix for four unknowns have?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFive: four coefficient columns and one constants column, regardless of the number of equations.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nEncode $4p+q=6$ and $-p+3q=2$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe augmented matrix is $\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}4&1&6\\\\-1&3&2\\end{array}\\right]$ in order p,q.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer encodes 2x+z=5 with coefficients [2,1]. What is missing?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nWith variable order x,y,z the coefficient row must be [2,0,1]. Omitting the zero would change the meaning of the second column.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA laboratory has unknown amounts r,s,t of three mixtures and observations r+2t=11 and 3r−s=4. Encode this data for a solver.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nWith order r,s,t use augmented rows [1,0,2|11] and [3,−1,0|4]. The missing terms are stored as zeros so that the same unknown keeps the same column.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nEncoding is useful when a computer accepts arrays rather than symbolic equations. A formative teaching check is to have a peer decode the matrix without seeing the original equations.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes adding a new equation add a column or a row?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIt adds a row. Adding a new unknown adds a coefficient column.\n:::end"
