        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nDistinguish one solution, no solution and infinitely many solutions using equation relationships and geometric meaning.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview arithmetic with signed numbers, substitution, ordered pairs and the distributive law. Keep variable names and units consistent when translating a context into equations.\n\n## Intuition first\nTwo instructions can meet at one choice, repeat the same constraint, or contradict each other. Before calculating many steps, ask whether the information is genuinely new and compatible.\n\n## Why this works\nElementary row operations preserve all solutions. Elimination can reveal an independent constraint, an identity with no new information, or a contradiction. A zero equation is satisfied by every tuple and does not itself force unknowns to zero. A contradiction with zero coefficients and a nonzero constant is satisfied by no tuple. For real linear systems, consistency together with free variables gives infinitely many solutions; consistency without free variables gives one solution.\n\n## Visual exploration\nSketch intersecting lines, parallel distinct lines and coincident lines as three separate diagrams. Label their common-point sets rather than assigning a solution merely from how many equations were written.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Three possible solution stories\nClassify these systems: (i) $x+y=11$, $x-y=3$; (ii) $x+y=11$, $2x+2y=22$; (iii) $x+y=11$, $2x+2y=25$. Give a solution description when one exists.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIn (i), adding gives 2x=14, so (x,y)=(7,4), a unique solution. In (ii), the second equation is twice the first; choose y=t and write x=11−t for any real t. In (iii), subtracting twice the first equation from the second gives 0=3, a contradiction, so no solution exists. The identity 0=0 in (ii) removes a redundant equation but does not remove the remaining constraint.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nCount independent constraints and check consistency rather than assuming that two equations always determine one point.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not treat 0=0 as x=0. A zero coefficient row with a nonzero augmented entry is a contradiction. “No unique solution” does not distinguish no solutions from infinitely many.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nCan a real linear system have exactly two different solutions and no others?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. If two distinct solutions exist, every affine combination along the line joining them is also a solution, giving infinitely many.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nClassify x+y=8 and 3x+3y=24.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe second equation repeats the first, so the system has infinitely many solutions: y=t and x=8−t for real t.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer calls x+y=8 and 3x+3y=26 an infinite-solution system because the left sides are multiples.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe constants must scale as well. Subtracting three times the first equation gives 0=2, so the system is inconsistent.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nTwo sensors report the same total resource as r+s=15 and 2r+2s=31. What does the linear model say about those reports?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThey cannot both hold exactly: subtracting twice the first report gives 0=1. This establishes inconsistency of the exact model, not which sensor is faulty.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nUse repeated and conflicting measurements to separate redundancy from inconsistency. A PCK suggestion is to require the words “compatible” and “independent” before learners count equations.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes an extra equation always reduce the solution set?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. A redundant equation may leave it unchanged; an incompatible equation may make it empty.\n:::end"
