        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nApply each elementary row operation to an entire augmented row and explain why its inverse preserves the solution set.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview arithmetic with signed numbers, substitution, ordered pairs and the distributive law. Keep variable names and units consistent when translating a context into equations.\n\n## Intuition first\nChanging how a constraint is written can make a system easier to read without changing what it permits. The key is reversibility: the old constraints must be recoverable from the new ones.\n\n## Why this works\nThe allowed operations are swapping two rows, multiplying one row by a nonzero scalar, and adding a scalar multiple of one row to another. Their inverses are respectively the same swap, scaling by the reciprocal, and adding the negative multiple. Every entry in an augmented row participates, including its constant. Scaling by zero is forbidden because it erases information and has no inverse.\n\n## Visual exploration\nUse a row ledger with “old row”, operation and “new row” columns. Highlight the augmented entry in the same colour as the rest of its row so it cannot be accidentally left unchanged.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise The three reversible row operations\nFor $M=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1&2&9\\\\3&-1&4\\end{array}\\right]$, perform $R_2\\leftarrow R_2-3R_1$. State the reverse operation and explain why solution tuples are preserved.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe new second row is [3−3,−1−6|4−27]=[0,−7|−23], so $M\\sim\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1&2&9\\\\0&-7&-23\\end{array}\\right]$. The first row is unchanged. The inverse is $R_2\\leftarrow R_2+3R_1$, which recovers every original entry. The transformed equations therefore impose exactly the original simultaneous constraints.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nRow equivalence concerns the same solution set, not equality of the displayed arrays. An operation log also supplies a reproducible verification path.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not scale by zero. Do not change one coefficient without changing the rest of that row. Row replacement changes the target row only, using the specified current source row.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhy must a row-scaling factor be nonzero?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nOnly a nonzero scalar has a multiplicative inverse; scaling by zero destroys a constraint.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nApply $R_1\\leftarrow-2R_1$ to [2,−3|5].\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nEvery entry scales: the new row is [−4,6|−10]. The inverse scales by −1/2.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer changes [1,2|9] to [2,4|9] while claiming to double the row.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe augmented entry must double too. The correct scaled row is [2,4|18].\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA computer solver swaps equation records to move a nonzero coefficient into its working position. Has it changed the physical constraints?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Reordering equations changes their display order, not the simultaneous conditions. The same row swap reverses the operation.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA teaching suggestion is to ask learners to reverse each row operation before accepting it as solution-preserving. This probes understanding more deeply than memorising three names.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIf R₂ is replaced by R₂−3R₁, should R₁ also change?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Only the target row changes; modifying the source row as well would be a different operation.\n:::end"
