        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nVerify a candidate against every original equation and then check whether the modelling context adds admissibility conditions.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview arithmetic with signed numbers, substitution, ordered pairs and the distributive law. Keep variable names and units consistent when translating a context into equations.\n\n## Intuition first\nA promising answer from a calculation is still a candidate. Test it against the original constraints before giving it a physical interpretation or trusting a software display.\n\n## Why this works\nSubstitution evaluates the original equation residuals. All residuals must be zero for an exact solution. A candidate can satisfy one equation while failing another, and a row-operation error can produce a plausible-looking tuple. After algebraic verification, a modelling problem may add nonnegativity, integrality or units; these conditions are not automatically supplied by an unconstrained real linear solver. Keep exact and approximate checks distinct.\n\n## Visual exploration\nCreate a table with one column for each original equation, placing the candidate’s left-side value beside the required right-side value. Use a final row to list any contextual restrictions.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Verification before interpretation\nFor $2x+y=13$ and $x-y=2$, test the candidates (5,3) and (4,5). Explain why checking just the first equation would be insufficient.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nFor (5,3), the left sides are 2·5+3=13 and 5−3=2, so both equations hold. For (4,5), the first gives 13 but the second gives −1 rather than 2. Thus only the first candidate solves the system. If x and y represent item counts, its nonnegative integer components also satisfy that additional context.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nAlgebraic validity and contextual admissibility are separate tests. A residual identifies a failed constraint without by itself explaining where the computational error arose.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not verify only the final transformed system after a possibly incorrect operation. A decimal close to the target is an approximation check, not an exact equality.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat residual must an exact equation check produce?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nZero: subtracting the required right side from the evaluated left side must give zero for every equation.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nTest (2,7) in x+2y=16 and 3x−y=−1.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe checks give 2+14=16 and 6−7=−1, so the tuple satisfies both equations.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer accepts (4,5) in the main example after checking only 2x+y=13.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe second equation fails: 4−5=−1, not 2. One successful equation does not establish a simultaneous solution.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA chemical-allocation model returns x=−2 and y=9 with exact zero residuals, but x represents litres allocated. Can the result be accepted unchanged?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. It may solve the real linear equations, but a negative allocation violates the context’s nonnegative-volume requirement. Review the model or feasibility rather than changing the number silently.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nUse a model with counts or resource amounts to distinguish solving from validating. A formative PCK check is to ask which failed requirement is algebraic and which is contextual.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes satisfying all equations automatically make a tuple physically meaningful?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. The context can impose additional restrictions not encoded in the equations.\n:::end"
