        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nPlan a short elimination route, distinguish fixed coefficients from unknowns and state when a parameter changes the solution story.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview arithmetic with signed numbers, substitution, ordered pairs and the distributive law. Keep variable names and units consistent when translating a context into equations.\n\n## Intuition first\nKnowing a system is linear narrows the available structure, but it does not promise a unique answer. A fixed parameter can change whether an equation contributes independent information.\n\n## Why this works\nAn expression may be linear in chosen unknowns even when its coefficients contain a fixed parameter. During solution, do not divide by that parameter without considering whether it can be zero. Instead split the cases associated with a potentially vanishing pivot. Equation addition and subtraction are tools for exposing compatibility; they are not commands to force a division in every case. A correct conclusion includes all permitted parameter cases.\n\n## Visual exploration\nSketch two lines whose slopes depend on a slider-like parameter k. Describe how they could intersect, become parallel or coincide; this is a conceptual manual diagram rather than an implemented widget.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Linearity is not one universal solution method\nConsider $x+y=10$ and $x+ky=16$, where k is a fixed real parameter. Determine the solution when $k\\ne1$ and classify the system when k=1.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nSubtract the first equation from the second: $(k-1)y=6$. If k≠1, then $y=6/(k-1)$ and $x=10-6/(k-1)$, giving one solution. If k=1, subtraction gives 0=6, so the system is inconsistent. Dividing by k−1 before this case split would lose the exceptional case.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA coefficient parameter is not necessarily an additional unknown to solve for. Exceptional cases should be stated explicitly rather than hidden inside a denominator.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not divide by an expression that may vanish without recording its nonzero case. Do not assume equal coefficient rows imply equal augmented rows.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nIs x+ky=16 linear in x,y when k is fixed?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nYes. The coefficients 1 and k are fixed with respect to the chosen unknowns x and y.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nSolve the main system when k=3.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe difference gives 2y=6, so y=3 and x=7. Both original equations hold.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer substitutes k=1 into y=6/(k−1) and calls the answer infinity.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThat formula was derived only for k≠1. At k=1 the equations contradict each other, so there is no solution, not an infinite coordinate.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nTwo nonnegative prices a,b satisfy a+b=18 and a+kb=24 for a fixed real calibration parameter k. Determine all k that admit a feasible price pair, including any boundary case with a free item. Explain why algebraic solvability alone is insufficient.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nSubtract the first equation from the second to obtain (k−1)b=6. At k=1 the equations are inconsistent. For k≠1, b=6/(k−1) and a=18−6/(k−1). If k<1, b is negative and violates feasibility. For k>1, b is positive; the additional condition a≥0 requires 18(k−1)≥6, hence k≥4/3. Thus the exact feasible set is k≥4/3. At k=4/3 the prices are a=0 and b=18, which is permitted because prices were specified as nonnegative rather than strictly positive. For 1<k<4/3, the unique algebraic solution has a<0 and must be rejected as a price model. The feasibility inequalities select a subset of the algebraically solvable parameter cases.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA PCK teaching suggestion is to contrast a parameter case split with a mechanical division. Ask learners what information a denominator restriction is preserving.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nWould replacing the second constant 16 by 10 change the k=1 case?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nYes. Then the equations coincide at k=1 and have infinitely many solutions instead of a contradiction.\n:::end"
