        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nConstruct row echelon form and justify pivot movement, zero rows and permitted row operations.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview augmented-matrix encoding, all three reversible row operations and the meaning of simultaneous satisfaction. Be able to check one candidate in every original equation.\n\n## Intuition first\nElimination organises equations so that each new row begins with information not already handled above it. A staircase of leading entries makes that structure visible.\n\n## Why this works\nIn row echelon form, all zero rows lie below nonzero rows, each leading nonzero entry lies strictly to the right of the one above it, and entries below a leading entry are zero. A leading entry is a pivot; it need not equal one in general echelon form. Swap rows when a working position is zero but a lower row can supply a nonzero entry. Only reversible row operations preserve the system.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw a staircase around the first nonzero entry of each row. Shade entries below each pivot and check that their zeros agree with the staircase positions.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Pivots and row echelon form\nReduce $\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&2&-1&-3\\\\2&5&1&2\\\\-1&1&2&3\\end{array}\\right]$ to row echelon form, recording every row replacement.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nApply $R_2\\leftarrow R_2-2R_1$ and $R_3\\leftarrow R_3+R_1$. This gives rows [1,2,−1|−3], [0,1,3|8], [0,3,1|0]. Next use $R_3\\leftarrow R_3-3R_2$ to obtain $\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&2&-1&-3\\\\0&1&3&8\\\\0&0&-8&-24\\end{array}\\right]$. The pivots step through the three coefficient columns. This is echelon form even though the final pivot is −8 rather than one.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nEchelon form is an organised intermediate description, not automatically the final list of unknown values. Pivot positions refer to coefficient columns when classifying unknowns.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not demand unit pivots for ordinary echelon form. Do not skip a row swap when a zero would otherwise be used as a divisor. Move zero rows below nonzero rows.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nMust every pivot in REF be one?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Unit pivots are required in reduced row echelon form, while ordinary echelon form permits any nonzero leading entry.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nIs $\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&0\\\\0&3&4\\\\0&0&0\\end{bmatrix}$ in REF?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nYes. The nonzero rows are above the zero row, the second pivot is to the right of the first, and entries below each pivot are zero.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer calls rows [0,1,2] and [1,0,3] an echelon arrangement in that order.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe second leading entry lies to the left of the first. Swap the rows to obtain the required increasing pivot-column order.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA data solver encounters a zero first coefficient in its top equation but a nonzero first coefficient below it. How can it continue without changing the constraints?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nSwap the appropriate rows, using the lower nonzero entry as the next pivot. The operation is reversible and changes only equation order.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA formative PCK check is to ask learners to identify the precise failed REF condition in a proposed staircase, rather than merely say it “looks wrong.”\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes eliminating below a pivot normally change the pivot row itself?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Row replacement changes the target row; retaining the pivot row makes subsequent elimination well defined.\n:::end"
