        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nIdentify free columns and express every solution using parameters with an explicit allowed range.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview augmented-matrix encoding, all three reversible row operations and the meaning of simultaneous satisfaction. Be able to check one candidate in every original equation.\n\n## Intuition first\nNot every constraint collection fixes every choice. Parameters describe the remaining freedom without pretending that an unconstrained variable is zero.\n\n## Why this works\nA coefficient column without a pivot corresponds to a free variable when the system is consistent. Assign an independent parameter to each free variable, then solve the pivot equations in terms of those parameters. Parameters range over the relevant scalar domain, here the real numbers. A complete parametric answer must produce every solution and only solutions, which can be checked by substitution and by showing that every possible free-variable choice is covered.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw a number line for one free parameter. Associate each selected parameter value with a complete tuple, making clear that the parameter is a coordinate choice rather than a new equation.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Free variables and parametric solutions\nDescribe all solutions of $\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1&0&2&5\\\\0&1&-3&-4\\\\0&0&0&0\\end{array}\\right]$ in variable order x,y,z.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe pivot columns are the x and y columns; z is free. Set z=t for any real t. The pivot equations give x=5−2t and y=−4+3t, so $(x,y,z)=(5-2t,-4+3t,t)$. For example, t=0 gives (5,−4,0), while t=1 gives (3,−1,1). Substitution verifies both equations for every real t, and every solution has some z=t.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA free variable is a genuine degree of choice, not an omitted calculation. Different parameter names can describe the same solution set.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not set free variables to zero unless only one particular solution is requested. Do not give the pivot variables independently of the parameters that link them.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhich column in the main matrix is free?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe third coefficient column has no pivot, so z is free. The augmented column is not an unknown column.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFor x+4y=9 with y free, give a parametric solution.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nSet y=t with t real. Then x=9−4t, so every solution is (9−4t,t).\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer reports (5,−4,0) as the complete answer to the main system.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThat tuple is one particular solution. The complete answer must retain z=t and its corresponding x and y expressions.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA resource model leaves one unrestricted real control variable after elimination. What extra information would select one operating point?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nA further compatible independent constraint or a justified optimisation/admissibility criterion may select one point. Choosing a parameter value silently would add an unsupported assumption.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA formative PCK activity is to ask learners to generate two different tuples from one parameter formula, then explain why listing two tuples is still not the whole set.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes one zero row force infinitely many solutions?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNot by itself. Count free coefficient columns after checking consistency; a redundant row can occur even when every unknown is a pivot variable.\n:::end"
