        "title": "Homogeneous systems and the zero solution",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "homogeneous systems",
          "trivial solution",
          "null solutions"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nExplain why a homogeneous system is always consistent and describe its nonzero solutions when free variables exist.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview augmented-matrix encoding, all three reversible row operations and the meaning of simultaneous satisfaction. Be able to check one candidate in every original equation.\n\n## Intuition first\nA balanced constraint with zero right side asks which input changes cancel each other. The all-zero choice always balances it, but it may not be the only one.\n\n## Why this works\nA homogeneous system has the form Ax=0. Substituting the zero vector proves consistency immediately. Row operations keep the right side zero, so elimination cannot create a nonzero augmented contradiction from valid operations. Nontrivial solutions exist exactly when there is at least one free variable. Over the real numbers, nonzero scalar multiples and sums of homogeneous solutions are again solutions because matrix multiplication is linear.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw arrows for two candidate cancellation directions from the origin. Explain that their linear combinations remain in the solution set; unlike a general nonhomogeneous solution set, this set contains the origin.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Homogeneous systems and the zero solution\nFind all real solutions of $x+2y+3z=0$, $2x+4y+6z=0$, and exhibit a nonzero solution.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe second equation is redundant. Choose y=s and z=t, so x=−2s−3t. Thus $(x,y,z)=s(-2,1,0)+t(-3,0,1)$ for real s,t. The zero solution corresponds to s=t=0. Taking s=1,t=0 gives the nonzero solution (−2,1,0), and substitution checks both original equations.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nThe trivial solution proves consistency, not uniqueness. A nontrivial solution is a nonzero vector, which may still contain some zero components.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not call every zero-right-side system uniquely solvable. Do not interpret “nontrivial” as requiring every component to be nonzero.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nCan a valid homogeneous system be inconsistent?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Its zero vector satisfies every equation, regardless of the coefficient matrix.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nGive one nonzero solution of 3x+y=0.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nChoose x=1, then y=−3. The vector (1,−3) is nonzero and satisfies the equation.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer rejects (−2,1,0) as nontrivial because one component is zero.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNontrivial means the whole vector is not the zero vector; one or more zero components are allowed.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA conservation model asks which changes leave every measured total unchanged, producing AΔx=0. What do its free variables mean?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThey describe independent adjustable changes invisible to those conservation measurements. The zero change is always possible; free variables permit nonzero balancing changes as well.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA PCK teaching suggestion is to compare the words “has a solution” and “has a nontrivial solution” before introducing abstract vector-space terminology.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nIf every coefficient column has a pivot, what homogeneous solution remains?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nOnly the zero solution remains because there are no free variables to produce another one.\n:::end"
