        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nConvert a reduced matrix into a complete vector-parametric answer and verify its particular and homogeneous parts.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview augmented-matrix encoding, all three reversible row operations and the meaning of simultaneous satisfaction. Be able to check one candidate in every original equation.\n\n## Intuition first\nA solution set can be organised as one reference solution plus every permitted balancing change. This is a more informative description than a list of sample tuples.\n\n## Why this works\nFor a consistent nonhomogeneous system, choose one particular solution by assigning the free parameters convenient values. Differences between solutions satisfy the corresponding homogeneous system because the right sides cancel. Consequently every solution can be written as a particular solution plus a homogeneous solution. This statement requires consistency: an inconsistent system has no particular solution to start from. Pivot equations provide the component relations needed to build both parts.\n\n## Visual exploration\nUse a four-column variable table showing which coordinates are pivot-controlled and which are parameter choices. Draw arrows from each free column into the pivot equations to track the resulting dependencies.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Read the whole solution set\nFor variables $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$, read all solutions of $\\left[\\begin{array}{cccc|c}1&2&0&-1&4\\\\0&0&1&3&7\\\\0&0&0&0&0\\end{array}\\right]$ as a particular vector plus homogeneous directions.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nLet $x_2=s$ and $x_4=t$. Then $x_1=4-2s+t$ and $x_3=7-3t$. The solution is $(4,0,7,0)+s(-2,1,0,0)+t(1,0,-3,1)$. The first vector satisfies the right sides 4 and 7; each direction vector satisfies the associated zero-right-side equations. Every real s,t gives a solution and every solution has these free coordinates.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nParameters describe the entire set only when every pivot dependency and the allowed parameter range are stated. The particular/homogeneous split is useful but not unique.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not add the same right-side constants to homogeneous direction vectors. Do not assume a particular solution is the only one merely because it has simple free-coordinate values.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nHow many free variables occur in the main reduced matrix?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nTwo: the second and fourth coefficient columns have no pivots.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nTake s=1,t=2 in the main solution description.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe tuple is (4−2+2,1,7−6,2)=(4,1,1,2). Substitution gives 4+2−2=4 and 1+6=7.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer gives the direction (4,0,7,0) as a homogeneous solution.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIt is the chosen particular solution and produces nonzero right sides. Homogeneous directions must produce zero right sides.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA network-flow model admits a baseline flow p and a balancing direction v with Av=0. Explain why p+tv remains a solution to Ap=b for every real t before capacity restrictions are imposed.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nLinearity gives A(p+tv)=Ap+tAv=b. Physical capacity bounds can restrict t, so the unconstrained algebraic family and the feasible operating family must be distinguished.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA teaching suggestion is to ask learners to verify the baseline and each direction separately, making the family structure easier to audit.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan a different particular solution describe the same family?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nYes. Changing the reference by a homogeneous solution leaves the full affine solution set unchanged.\n:::end"
