        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nCompute a rectangular product and explain why its inner and outer dimensions have different roles.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview row-column indexing, signed arithmetic, vector components and the meaning of an augmented coefficient table. Distinguish an array’s size from its collection of values.\n\n## Intuition first\nA product entry combines one output row with one input column. The shared inner index is summed away, leaving the row of the first matrix and the column of the second.\n\n## Why this works\nFor A of size m×n and B of size n×p, AB has size m×p. Its entry in row i and column j is the sum of A’s row-i entries multiplied by B’s column-j entries. The equal inner dimensions make that pairing possible. Matrix multiplication is not entrywise multiplication; a product can be rectangular or have a different size from either factor. Check dimensions before calculating any individual entry.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw arrows from one row of A and one column of B into a single product cell. Label the shared inner index and repeat the construction for a second cell.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Matrix multiplication as row-column pairing\nCompute $AB$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&-1\\\\3&0&4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2&1\\\\-1&3\\\\5&-2\\end{bmatrix}$. State the result’s size.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe sizes are 2×3 and 3×2, so AB is 2×2. Pair rows and columns:\n$$AB=\\begin{bmatrix}1(2)+2(-1)+(-1)5&1(1)+2(3)+(-1)(-2)\\\\3(2)+0(-1)+4(5)&3(1)+0(3)+4(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5&9\\\\26&-5\\end{bmatrix}.$$\nEach entry sums three products because the inner dimension is three.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nThe inner dimension controls how many products are added; the outer dimensions locate the result’s entries.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not multiply corresponding entries as if computing an entrywise product. Do not assume the output has the inner dimension as both of its dimensions.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat size results from a 4×2 matrix times a 2×5 matrix?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe inner sizes match, so the product is defined and has outer size 4×5.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind $\\begin{bmatrix}2&-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\\\3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe row-column sum is 2·4−1·3=5, represented as a 1×1 matrix.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer multiplies a 2×3 matrix by a 2×2 matrix because both have two rows.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe required match is the first factor’s column count with the second factor’s row count. Here 3≠2, so that product is undefined.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA programme stores a 3×2 student-by-task mark table and a 2×1 task-weight column. What does their product represent?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIt is a 3×1 column of weighted totals, one per student, provided each weight corresponds to the matching task column. Each output is a row-column weighted sum.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA teaching suggestion is to require a labelled product-cell diagram before arithmetic so the multiplication rule is tied to indexing rather than memorised motion.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes a defined AB guarantee that BA is defined?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Reversing factors imposes a different inner-dimension check.\n:::end"
