        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nTranspose rectangular arrays correctly and apply trace only to square matrices while preserving product order rules.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview row-column indexing, signed arithmetic, vector components and the meaning of an augmented coefficient table. Distinguish an array’s size from its collection of values.\n\n## Intuition first\nExchanging rows and columns changes how the same entries are indexed. A diagonal summary is a different operation and has a narrower domain.\n\n## Why this works\nTransposition exchanges row and column positions, turning an m×n array into an n×m array. The transpose of a sum is the sum of transposes, while the transpose of a product reverses factor order: (AB)ᵀ=BᵀAᵀ. That reversal follows from the row-column indexing of each product entry. Trace is the sum of main-diagonal entries of a square matrix; it is linear but not generally multiplicative. Do not apply a square-only definition to a rectangular matrix.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw the main diagonal as a fold line for a square array, and use labelled row/column positions rather than a physical fold for a rectangular one. Highlight the diagonal entries used by trace.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Transpose and trace with conditions\nFor $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2&-1\\\\3&0&4\\end{bmatrix}$, find Aᵀ. For $C=\\begin{bmatrix}4&-2\\\\3&7\\end{bmatrix}$, find tr(C) and compare tr(C²) with [tr(C)]².\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe transpose is $A^T=\\begin{bmatrix}1&3\\\\2&0\\\\-1&4\\end{bmatrix}$. For C, tr(C)=4+7=11. Multiplication gives $C^2=\\begin{bmatrix}10&-22\\\\33&43\\end{bmatrix}$, so tr(C²)=53, whereas [tr(C)]²=121. These unequal values show that trace is not a multiplicative rule.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nTranspose preserves entries while changing their positions and dimensions. Trace compresses a square array to one scalar and loses information.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not leave dimensions unchanged when transposing a rectangular array. Do not replace (AB)ᵀ by AᵀBᵀ. Do not infer matrix equality from equal traces.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat is the size of the transpose of a 3×5 matrix?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIt is 5×3, because rows and columns exchange roles.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nFind the trace of $\\begin{bmatrix}6&1\\\\-4&2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe trace is 6+2=8; off-diagonal entries do not contribute.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer writes (AB)ᵀ=AᵀBᵀ for arbitrary compatible factors.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe correct identity reverses order: (AB)ᵀ=BᵀAᵀ. A dimensional check can already reveal the wrong order for rectangular factors.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA marks table is student-by-task. What does its transpose represent?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIt is task-by-student: the same recorded values are reindexed so a former column becomes a row. It is not a new set of measurements.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA PCK suggestion is to compare transpose’s full reindexing with trace’s information loss before teaching their symbolic identities.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes a zero trace force a square matrix to be the zero matrix?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Diagonal entries can cancel, and off-diagonal entries are not measured by trace.\n:::end"
