        "title": "Identity, zero and failed scalar analogies",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "identity matrices",
          "zero products",
          "idempotent matrices"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nUse dimension-matched identity and zero matrices and reject invalid cancellation or zero-product assumptions.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview row-column indexing, signed arithmetic, vector components and the meaning of an augmented coefficient table. Distinguish an array’s size from its collection of values.\n\n## Intuition first\nAn action that does nothing and an action that sends everything to zero are different. Matrices representing them have different algebraic roles.\n\n## Why this works\nA square identity matrix leaves compatible vectors unchanged. For an m×n matrix A, the identities IₘA=A and AIₙ=A use potentially different sizes. A zero matrix is defined by its dimensions as well as its zero entries. Nonzero matrices can multiply to zero because one factor’s output can fall in a direction erased by the other. Therefore scalar zero-product and cancellation arguments cannot be transferred without additional hypotheses such as an appropriate inverse.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw a two-stage action in which the first stage produces a nonzero vector that the next stage maps to zero. This explains why a zero combined output need not mean either whole transformation is zero.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Identity, zero and failed scalar analogies\nFor $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\\\-1&0\\end{bmatrix}$, compute AB and A². Explain why neither result permits ordinary scalar-style cancellation.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nMultiplication gives $AB=\\begin{bmatrix}0&0\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}$ even though A and B are both nonzero. Also $A^2=\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}=A$. A is idempotent, but A²=A does not imply A=I because A is not invertible and cannot simply be cancelled.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nA matrix’s action on directions explains algebraic exceptions better than treating arrays as enlarged numbers. Check hypotheses before cancelling a factor.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not conclude A=0 or B=0 from AB=0. Do not cancel a factor without an appropriate inverse. Match identity dimensions on the correct side.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nFor a 2×3 matrix A, what sizes do left and right identities have?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe left identity is 2×2 and the right identity is 3×3.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nCompute $\\begin{bmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe output is (0,0), although the input vector is nonzero.\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer cancels A from A²=A and concludes A=I for the main A.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nCancellation would require invertibility. This nonidentity matrix provides a direct counterexample to that unsupported step.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA data-processing stage discards one feature. Can a nonzero change in the discarded feature disappear from the final output?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nYes. A nonzero input in an erased direction can map to zero, which is why a zero output alone does not imply a zero input.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA formative PCK activity can ask for one counterexample before accepting a scalar-style law as a matrix law.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nDoes AI=A establish that A has an inverse?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nNo. Every compatible A satisfies the identity law, including singular matrices.\n:::end"
