        "title": "Undo a product in reverse order",
        "visual": null,
        "concepts": [
          "inverse of a product",
          "noncommutativity",
          "reverse composition"
        ],
        "markdown": "## Learning goal\nDerive the inverse of a product by preserving order and verify a concrete counterexample to the wrong order.\n\n## Prerequisites\nReview compatible matrix multiplication, identity matrices, associativity, noncommutativity and system consistency. Be able to verify a product entry by entry before using inverse notation.\n\n## Intuition first\nIf one action is followed by another, undo the last action first. Matrix algebra records exactly that reverse sequence.\n\n## Why this works\nFor invertible square A and B of the same size, (AB)⁻¹=B⁻¹A⁻¹. Check both products: (AB)(B⁻¹A⁻¹)=A(BB⁻¹)A⁻¹=I and the reversed verification works similarly. The argument uses associativity to bring matching inverses together; it does not assume that arbitrary factors commute. For a longer invertible product, reverse the entire factor sequence when inverting.\n\n## Visual exploration\nDraw x→Bx→A(Bx), then arrows returning through A⁻¹ followed by B⁻¹. Read the total return product from right to left on a column vector.\n\n## Procedure\n### Original worked example\n:::exercise Undo a product in reverse order\nLet $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3&0\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$. Compute (AB)⁻¹ using reverse order and compare with the generally incorrect A⁻¹B⁻¹.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nHere $AB=\\begin{bmatrix}3&4\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$. The inverse factors are $A^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1&-2\\\\0&1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $B^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1/3&0\\\\0&1/2\\end{bmatrix}$. Therefore $B^{-1}A^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1/3&-2/3\\\\0&1/2\\end{bmatrix}$, which multiplies with AB to give I₂. The wrong order gives $A^{-1}B^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1/3&-1\\\\0&1/2\\end{bmatrix}$, a different matrix.\n:::end\n\n## Interpretation\nReverse order is structural rather than a mnemonic accident: it is what makes adjacent inverse pairs cancel.\n\n## Common mistakes\nDo not reverse factors in the original product while pretending only to regroup them. Do not assume the inverse factors commute because each is an inverse.\n\n## Assessment\n### Recognition\n:::exercise Recognition\nWhat is the inverse of ABC when all three factors are invertible and compatible?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nIt is C⁻¹B⁻¹A⁻¹, reversing the full factor sequence.\n:::end\n\n### Procedure\n#### Original worked example\n:::exercise Procedure\nUsing the main correct inverse, compute (AB)⁻¹(7,3).\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe result is (7/3−2,3/2)=(1/3,3/2). Multiplying by AB recovers (7,3).\n:::end\n\n### Error correction\n:::exercise Error correction\nA peer claims (AB)⁻¹=A⁻¹B⁻¹ because inversion distributes without changing order.\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nThe cancellation proof requires reverse order. The concrete factors above give different matrices, so the claimed universal rule is false.\n:::end\n\n### Transfer\n:::exercise Transfer\nA message is scrambled by B and then by A. In what sequence must a decoder apply the known undoing actions?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nApply A⁻¹ first to undo the latest action, then B⁻¹. On column vectors the total decoder is B⁻¹A⁻¹.\n:::end\n\n## Application\nA formative PCK activity can connect a two-step physical action with the algebraic cancellation proof and require learners to name every regrouping.\n\nUse the common-mistake discussion as a misconception probe and the questions as formative checks.\n\n## Pause and Predict\n:::exercise Pause and Predict\nCan the wrong-order expression happen to be right for special factors?\n:::answer\n**Worked solution:**\nYes, if those particular inverses commute. That special case does not justify the general formula.\n:::end"
